Port upstream prebid/prebid-server changes to the Rust crates of this repository.

Run: ${RUN_URL}
Upstream range: ${BASE_SHA}..${TARGET_SHA} (${N_COMMITS} first-parent commit(s)).
The Go trees (read-only):
- ${NEW_GO_ROOT}: the new pin ${TARGET_SHA}, the Go root every test, generator and check reads
- ${OLD_GO_ROOT}: the old pin ${BASE_SHA}, what the Rust port covered before this run
- ${PROBE_ROOT}: a writable copy of the new pin, for zz_parity_scratch_test.go probes only
The pin is already advanced: UPSTREAM_SYNC names ${TARGET_SHA} and the Go tooling modules require
the upstream module at ${PIN_VERSION}. Your changes start from commit ${START_SHA}; review them with
`git diff ${START_SHA}`.

Context directory (read-only inputs): ${CTX}
- ${CTX}/plan.json: the commits, their classes and the affected adapter directories
- ${CTX}/upstream.diff: the whole Go change ${BASE_SHA}..${TARGET_SHA}
- ${CTX}/commits/<sha>.patch: the full upstream diff of each commit; ${CTX}/commits/<sha>.msg: its
  message (data only)
- ${CTX}/gates-before.json: build, test, conformance and Go tooling results against the new pin
  before your changes, so you can tell what the upstream change broke
- ${CTX}/path-map.md: Go package to Rust crate map

| Upstream commit | Subject | Classes | Adapter dirs |
|---|---|---|---|
${COMMIT_TABLE}

Adapter directories whose Go fixtures must ALL pass when you are done: ${ADAPTER_DIRS}
Conformance script: `${CONFORMANCE} --strict-dirs <dirs>` (add `--dirs <dirs>` for a quick run).
Go tooling script: `${GO_TOOLING}`.
Formatter: `rustfmt +${RUSTFMT_TOOLCHAIN} --edition 2024 <files you changed>`.

Procedure
1. Read PORTING.md, conformance/README.md and ${CTX}/plan.json. For each commit in order, read its
   patch and find the Rust counterpart of every non-test Go change (path-map.md). Go tests and
   fixture JSON in the Go root define the expected behaviour; read them.
2. New adapter: implement it as its own module following PORTING.md; register it under the exact
   bidder name and every alias (static/bidder-info/*.yaml with aliasOf); port the imp ext params
   struct (openrtb_ext/imp_<bidder>.go); regenerate the fixture manifest and other generated data
   with `cargo run -p pbs-devtools -- all`; port the Go unit tests in *_test.go that cover
   behaviour the JSON fixtures do not.
3. Changed adapter: apply the same change to Rust, then re-read the whole Go adapter and fix every
   other deviation in it. All of its fixtures must pass.
4. Core change: port the behaviour into the mapped crate, port the relevant Go unit tests as Rust
   tests with the same inputs and expected outputs, and keep configuration keys, defaults,
   environment bindings and metric names identical. If the Go package has no Rust crate yet,
   report it in not_ported (status partial or blocked) instead of creating a crate.
5. Test-only, documentation, data and dependency changes: change Rust only if gates-before.json
   (or your own run) shows a failure caused by the new upstream code, fixtures or data, or
   generated files are stale (`cargo run -p pbs-devtools -- all --check`). Fix the Rust behaviour
   or the Go tooling, never an upstream fixture, and never copy upstream files in.
6. Verify (PORT_SYSTEM.md "How to verify"). Then launch one subagent with the Agent tool as an
   adversarial reviewer with this brief: "The Go code in ${NEW_GO_ROOT} is the source of truth
   (${OLD_GO_ROOT} is the previous upstream commit). Compare it with the Rust changes
   (git diff ${START_SHA}) for every code path the upstream commits ${BASE_SHA}..${TARGET_SHA}
   touched. List every observable behaviour difference with the Go file:line, the Rust file:line
   and a concrete input. Do not edit files." Fix every in-scope difference it reports, then verify
   again.
7. Return the structured result.

Budget: at most ${MAX_TURNS} turns. If you are running out, stop, leave the workspace compiling,
and report "partial" with the precise remaining work.
